INT. HALLORAN FAMILY GREENHOUSE - SATURDAY MORNING

A glass lean-to bolted to the back of a brick house. Tomato vines, hanging fern baskets, a rusted wheelbarrow. Condensation drips from every pane. A radio on a shelf plays low country music.

GRANDMA ROSALIND HALLORAN (70s, tiny, sunhat, hands like gnarled roots) kneels at a seed tray.

Beside her, TOBIAS "TOBY" HALLORAN (9, cowlick, one shoe untied) holds a trowel like a sword.

Near the door, DESMOND HALLORAN (40s, Toby's father, rumpled) drinks coffee from a thermos lid. Next to him, JUNIPER HALLORAN (15, black nail polish, earbuds in) scrolls her phone.

DESMOND
Toby, easy with that.

TOBY
I am easy.

ROSALIND
He is a delicate surgeon.

JUNIPER
He is a menace.

Toby jabs the trowel into the soil. It strikes something hard. CLINK.

TOBY
Gran! There's something!

ROSALIND
Stop digging.

TOBY
It's metal!

ROSALIND
Stop digging, Tobias.

Her voice is suddenly flat, brittle. Everyone goes still. Even the radio seems to hush.

DESMOND
Mum?

ROSALIND
Step back. Everyone.

She pushes herself up, wincing, takes Toby by the shoulders and moves him aside. She brushes dirt away with her thumb. A rusted tin lid appears, stamped with faded red roses.

JUNIPER
(pulling out one earbud)
Is that a body?

DESMOND
Juniper!

ROSALIND
It is not a body.

TOBY
Is it treasure?

ROSALIND
(quietly)
It is something I buried the year your grandfather left.

The room holds its breath. Desmond sets down the thermos lid.

DESMOND
Mum, you don't have to--

ROSALIND
I do. It's been forty years. I'm tired of tending around it.

She kneels again, slower. Works her fingers under the lid. It resists, then gives with a shriek of old metal.

Inside, wrapped in oilcloth, a bundle of letters tied with twine, a man's gold watch, and a child's baby shoe.

TOBY
That's tiny.

JUNIPER
That's Dad's.

Desmond stares at the shoe. He crouches slowly, picks it up. His thumb rubs the faded stitching.

DESMOND
You said you lost these.

ROSALIND
I said a lot of things.

DESMOND
(louder)
You told me he took everything!

ROSALIND
(louder)
I told you what I could bear to tell you!

The shout rings off the glass. Toby flinches. Juniper steps in front of him, protective, then looks mortified and steps back.

DESMOND
(to Rosalind)
Forty years.

ROSALIND
(to Desmond)
Forty years.

Silence. Condensation drips from a pane onto a fern frond. Plink.

Rosalind lifts the watch, wipes the face with her sleeve. Holds it to her ear.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
Still ticking.

DESMOND
It can't be.

ROSALIND
Your father always wound it twice a day. I think I kept doing it, in my head, after.

TOBY
(small voice)
Can I hold it?

Rosalind looks at Desmond. He nods, slow. She presses the watch into Toby's palm, folding his fingers around it.

ROSALIND
Gently, my darling. It has been waiting a long time.

Toby lifts it to his ear, eyes huge.

TOBY
It's like a tiny heart.

JUNIPER
(softly)
That is actually kind of sweet.

DESMOND
Don't tell anyone.

JUNIPER
I'll tell everyone.

The corner of Desmond's mouth twitches. He sits down hard on the overturned wheelbarrow and laughs, short and wet. Rosalind lets out a breath and laughs too. Juniper joins, helpless. Toby looks from face to face, confused and thrilled.

TOBY
What's funny?

ROSALIND
Absolutely nothing.

DESMOND
Absolutely nothing.

ROSALIND
Absolutely nothing.

JUNIPER ^
Everything.

The radio, as if on cue, turns to a slow waltz. Rosalind sets the tin in the sun, lid open, and goes back to her seed tray.

ROSALIND
Now. Who wants to plant some tomatoes?
